      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "Inclusive AI can play a transformative role in reducing healthcare and digital inequities by improving access for marginalized populations and addressing social determinants of health. By applying inclusive design frameworks, considering regulatory frameworks and participatory approaches during the development, inclusive AI can foster trust and transparency of AI solutions. Co-design methodologies and international collaborations in aggregating data sets to be used in AI systems may improve AI fairness. Educating stakeholders on AI applications and limitations can improve adoption and reduce skepticism. AI can improve in accuracy and personalization by refining models, improving data collection, and leveraging privacy-preserving learning techniques.",
      "reason": "The authors discuss actionable implications for practice: inclusive design, participatory approaches, co-design, stakeholder education, and technical steps (data collection, privacy-preserving techniques) to improve adoption and fairness in clinical use."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_23d_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_14",
      "quote": "We extracted several threats from the papers. Bias in AI algorithms, underrepresentation of marginalized groups, and exclusion of low-income regions contribute to unequal healthcare access and outcomes. ... AI-driven healthcare faces challenges related to data privacy, commercial exploitation, and the absence of clear consent mechanisms. Misuse of patient data to train AI models raises ethical and legal concerns, potentially eroding trust in AI applications. ... From these threats, the most commonly discussed threat identified from the selected and reviewed sources was the exacerbation of inequities, noted in 14 articles, warning that biases in algorithms and exclusion of low-income regions could further widen health disparities [5, 6, 8, 9, 22, 23, 26-31, 33, 34].",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly discusses policy-related implications: the need for regulatory frameworks, consent mechanisms, data privacy, and governance to mitigate biases and inequities at a systems/policy level."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_23d_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_19",
      "quote": "Future research should evaluate the effectiveness of the frameworks considered in real-world AI implementation. By identifying gaps and overlaps among existing frameworks, a more unified, comprehensive, and adaptable framework could be developed for inclusive AI in healthcare. Such standardized framework could address the weaknesses and threats identified in our analysis. ... Future research should also prioritize recommendations based on their feasibility and potential impact. At short term, regular actions such as conducting bias audits, implementing transparency measures, and enhancing data privacy measures could help to identify and mitigate unfairness in AI algorithms. At medium and long term, it would be desirable to adopt more user-centered approaches involving diverse stakeholders in order to create equitable AI systems. ... Further research should consider duplicate extraction to improve reliability. Moreover, issues related to the impact of AI in health care related to race and gender were not addressed in detail as this has been investigated in prior studies. ... Future research should explore how these findings translate to broader AI use cases further also considering the impact of AI use in healthcare related to race and gender.",
      "reason": "The authors provide explicit, detailed recommendations for future research: evaluating frameworks in real-world settings, developing a unified framework, prioritizing feasible recommendations, conducting bias audits, enhancing transparency/privacy, using user-centered approaches, improving methodological rigor (duplicate extraction), and studying race/gender impacts."
    }
  ],
